# GUIDED POSITION-CONTROLLER LIMITS, RAISED -- the arm for "can the flight controller follow ego's plan at all"
#
# WHY (measured, `docs/metrics/why-collide-20261016.txt` and `plan-quality-tracking-20261016.txt`):
#   * ArduPilot's GUIDED posvelaccel path does not fly the position we send.  It treats it as a GOAL for an
#     acceleration-limited reference model (`shape_pos_vel_accel_xy`, `AP_Math/control.cpp:346-400`) whose limit
#     comes from `WP_ACC` (old `WPNAV_ACCEL`) via `mode_guided.cpp:255/321`.
#   * `WP_ACC` default = 2.5 m/s2 (`AC_WPNav.h:15`), and the run realised 2.463 m/s2 -- while ego's plan asks for
#     p99 6.15 / max 13.84 m/s2.  The FCU's internal reference therefore trailed the plan by up to 0.591 m, and
#     `PSCN.TVN - PSCN.DVN = PSC_NE_POS_P x (DPN - PN)` proves the loop closes on THAT reference.
#   * 24.4 % of all 1664 setpoints demanded more than the 2.463 m/s2 the shaper would give.
#
# THE FOUR THAT BIND TOGETHER -- raising one alone does nothing:
#   WP_ACC         the shaper's horizontal acceleration limit      (2.5 -> 6.0 m/s2)
#   PSC_NE_JERK    how fast it may REACH that limit                (5.0 -> 30 m/s3: 6 m/s2 in 0.2 s, not 1.2 s)
#   ATC_ANGLE_MAX  the geometric ceiling, g*tan(tilt)              (30 -> 40 deg: 5.66 -> 8.2 m/s2)
#   WP_SPD         the shaper's horizontal SPEED limit             (2.0 -> 4.0 m/s; it was BELOW the 2.35 m/s
#                                                                    the plan commanded, so it was clipping too)
#
# TWO NAMES THAT LOOK RIGHT AND ARE NOT.  `ATC_ACCEL_P_MAX` / `ATC_ACCEL_R_MAX` do not exist in this firmware --
# 4.7 renamed them to **`ATC_ACC_R_MAX` / `ATC_ACC_P_MAX`** in deg/s/s (`AC_AttitudeControl.cpp:51,193,203`, and
# the built-in conversions at `:1189,1197` exist precisely because the old names are gone).  The first version of
# this file set the old names, and `_stage/live-params.py` caught it on the pad: the FCU answered "(no answer)"
# for both, i.e. a parameter that is silently ignored -- the same trap `tools/install-ego-launch.py --static`
# exists for on the launch side.  The attitude controller's roll/pitch acceleration is left at its default here
# ON PURPOSE: it is in deg/s/s and its default is already far above what a 40 deg tilt can use, so naming it
# would be a third variable with no mechanism behind it.
#
# MEASURED ON THE PAD, live over MAVLink (`_stage/verify-guided-limits.sh`, 2026-10-16):
#     control (the mission's own files, which set NONE of these):
#         WP_ACC 2.5000   PSC_NE_JERK 5.0000   ATC_ANGLE_MAX 30.0000   WP_SPD 2.0000
#     with this file appended last:
#         WP_ACC 6.0000   PSC_NE_JERK 30.0000  ATC_ANGLE_MAX 40.0000   WP_SPD 4.0000

#
# WHAT THIS DOES *NOT* FIX, and the reader must not expect it to (`why-collide-20261016.txt`):
#   * the REFERENCE STEPS at every 1 Hz replan (median 0.281 m, max 0.927 m on the wire; the sampled point jumps
#     because `t_cur = now - start_time` restarts) -- a more capable controller follows a jump HARDER;
#   * the plan's own 13.84 m/s2 peaks, which exceed even this file's ceiling;
#   * the 5-17 cm margin the plan gives itself on the tight stretch.
#   So this is one variable of a pair: the reference-continuity fix (review step 5) is the other, and the flight
#   that tests this file must measure the tracking error AND the reference steps, not just the outcome.
#
# FLOWN WITH: EXTRA_PARM=params/guided_limits_fast.parm   (run-mission.sh appends it LAST to --defaults; the
# path is printed in the revision block, so a sortie's own log names the arm it flew).
WP_ACC 6.0
PSC_NE_JERK 30.0
ATC_ANGLE_MAX 40.0
WP_SPD 4.0
